## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single entry point where all CMS content flows into the React tree. All 236+ consumers of useContent depend on this provider. It currently creates a static memoized context from defaultContent that never updates reactively — the exact gap that prevents Contentstack Live Preview from pushing real-time edits into the app. The provider must become reactive to live preview callbacks.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the static useMemo with useState initialized from defaultContent, and expose a setContent updater in the context value so that a Contentstack livePreview callback (registered at app bootstrap) can push updated content into the tree. Change: `const [content, setContent] = useState<IContentstackContent>(defaultContent);` and set context value to `{ content, setContent }`. Also update the IContentstackContent context type in the createContext call to include `setContent: (c: IContentstackContent) => void`. The livePreview callback (registered via Stack.livePreview from the Contentstack SDK at _app.tsx or equivalent bootstrap) then calls setContent with the updated content object, causing all useContent consumers to re-render with live-edited data.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When preview mode is active, the rendered page displays draft (unpublished) content from the CMS in place of the published version, visible to the tester on the page itself.
- When preview mode is not active, the rendered page displays the published content from the CMS.
- While preview mode is active, content edits saved in the CMS are reflected on the previewed page without the tester changing any application code or configuration.
- After a draft entry is updated in the CMS, reloading the preview page displays the updated draft content.
- When preview mode is deactivated, the page displays the published content rather than the previously previewed draft content.
- Upon deactivation of preview mode, the published content is displayed immediately on the next page view, with no stale draft content remaining visible.
- On the GO brand site, activating preview mode displays draft CMS content on the rendered page, and deactivating it restores the published content.
- On the UP brand site, activating preview mode displays draft CMS content on the rendered page, and deactivating it restores the published content.
- On the MX brand site, activating preview mode displays draft CMS content on the rendered page, and deactivating it restores the published content.
- Previewed draft content is rendered across all CMS-driven elements on the page — links, images, and tables — not just a single element type.

